---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Pregnancy Discrimination Damages Calculator | pregnancydiscriminationcalc.com';
const description = 'Estimate potential damages for pregnancy discrimination under the Pregnancy Discrimination Act and Title VII: back pay, compensatory, punitive damages. Free, informational.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Pregnancy Discrimination Damages Calculator</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <p>The Pregnancy Discrimination Act (PDA) of 1978 amended Title VII to prohibit discrimination based on pregnancy, childbirth, or related medical conditions. The Pregnant Workers Fairness Act (PWFA), effective June 2023, added an affirmative accommodation requirement. The PUMP Act (2022) extended lactation accommodation rights. Together, these federal laws protect workers at employers with 15 or more employees from a wide range of discriminatory conduct — including termination, demotion, failure to accommodate, denial of promotion, and hostile work environments — and provide meaningful financial remedies when violations occur. This calculator estimates your potential recovery.</p>

  <h2>Run the Estimate</h2>
  <form id="calc" novalidate>
    <label for="salary">Annual salary (USD)</label>
    <input id="salary" type="number" value="70000" min="0" />
    <label for="action">Adverse action type</label>
    <select id="action">
      <option value="termination">Termination</option>
      <option value="demotion">Demotion or pay reduction</option>
      <option value="failure-promote">Failure to promote</option>
      <option value="forced-leave">Forced unpaid leave or constructive discharge</option>
      <option value="accommodation">Failure to accommodate pregnancy-related restriction</option>
    </select>
    <label for="months">Months of lost employment (if terminated/demoted)</label>
    <input id="months" type="number" value="8" min="0" max="60" />
    <label for="employer-size">Employer size</label>
    <select id="employer-size">
      <option value="15-100">15–100 employees</option>
      <option value="101-200">101–200 employees</option>
      <option value="201-500">201–500 employees</option>
      <option value="500plus">500+ employees</option>
    </select>
    <label for="malice">Employer act with malice or reckless indifference?</label>
    <select id="malice">
      <option value="no">No / unclear</option>
      <option value="yes">Yes — documented animus toward pregnancy</option>
    </select>
    <button type="submit">Estimate damages</button>
  </form>
  <p id="result" aria-live="polite"></p>

  <h2>How Pregnancy Discrimination Damages Are Calculated</h2>
  <p>Pregnancy discrimination claims under Title VII follow a structured damages framework with several distinct components. Understanding each component helps you assess which aspects of your situation are most significant to your potential recovery.</p>

  <h3>Back Pay (Uncapped)</h3>
  <p>Back pay covers all wages and benefits lost from the date of the discriminatory act through the date of settlement or judgment. It is an equitable remedy, not subject to the Title VII damages caps. If you were terminated, back pay runs from your last day of employment through the date you found equivalent work — or longer if you have not yet been re-employed. If you were demoted or had your pay reduced, back pay is the difference between what you were paid and what you should have been paid for the applicable period. Courts reduce back pay by amounts earned or that could have been earned through reasonable job search effort.</p>

  <h3>Compensatory and Punitive Damages (Capped)</h3>
  <p>The Civil Rights Act of 1991 added compensatory and punitive damages to the Title VII remedial scheme, subject to per-employer-size caps. Compensatory damages cover emotional distress, pain and suffering, humiliation, and loss of enjoyment of life — non-economic harm caused by the discrimination. Punitive damages require proof that the employer acted with malice or reckless indifference to your federally protected rights, as established in Kolstad v. American Dental Association, 527 U.S. 526 (1999). The combined compensatory and punitive cap ranges from $50,000 (smallest covered employers) to $300,000 (500+ employees).</p>

  <h3>Front Pay (Uncapped)</h3>
  <p>When reinstatement is not feasible — because the relationship has broken down, because no comparable position is available, or because the work environment would be hostile — courts award front pay to compensate for future earnings lost due to the discrimination. Front pay is an equitable remedy determined by the judge, not the jury, and is not subject to the Title VII cap. Duration depends on your age, career stage, the local labor market, and the likelihood of finding equivalent employment.</p>

  <h3>Attorney Fees</h3>
  <p>Title VII &sect; 706(k) provides that prevailing plaintiffs are entitled to attorney fees and costs from the defendant employer. This means that employment attorneys take pregnancy discrimination cases on contingency because their compensation comes from the employer, separately from and in addition to your damages. This makes even moderate-value claims economically viable to pursue.</p>

  <h2>Learn More</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/how-pregnancy-discrimination-damages-work">How pregnancy discrimination damages work</a></li>
    <li><a href="/guides/types-of-pregnancy-discrimination">Types of pregnancy discrimination</a></li>
    <li><a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-pregnancy-discrimination">What to do after pregnancy discrimination</a></li>
    <li><a href="/guides/pregnancy-discrimination-misconceptions">Common misconceptions</a></li>
    <li><a href="/case-types">Case types overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="/methodology">Calculator methodology</a></li>
    <li><a href="/faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
  </ul>

  <script>
    document.getElementById('calc').addEventListener('submit', e => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const salary = parseFloat(document.getElementById('salary').value)||70000;
      const months = parseFloat(document.getElementById('months').value)||0;
      const empSize = document.getElementById('employer-size').value;
      const malice = document.getElementById('malice').value==='yes';
      const action = document.getElementById('action').value;
      const caps = {'15-100':50000,'101-200':100000,'201-500':200000,'500plus':300000};
      const cap = caps[empSize];
      const backPay = (salary/12)*months;
      const comp = malice ? cap*0.6 : cap*0.3;
      const punitive = malice ? cap*0.4 : 0;
      const frontPay = (action==='termination'||action==='forced-leave') ? salary*0.5 : 0;
      const total = backPay + comp + punitive + frontPay;
      const fmt = n=>'$'+Math.round(n).toLocaleString();
      document.getElementById('result').innerHTML =
        `<strong>Estimated range: ${fmt(total*0.6)} &ndash; ${fmt(total*1.3)}</strong><br>Back pay: ${fmt(backPay)} &bull; Compensatory: ${fmt(comp)} &bull; Punitive: ${fmt(punitive)} &bull; Front pay: ${fmt(frontPay)}<br><em>Title VII caps combined compensatory + punitive at ${fmt(cap)} for your employer size. Back pay and front pay are uncapped. Attorney fees recoverable separately.</em>`;
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
